  "query_preview": "..(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 600 AM EDT WED SEP 23 2026\n\nThe long-term period opens Thursday evening with a closed ~579 dam\nlow near/over the Outer Banks, supporting a strong early autumn \nstorm system developing off the East Coast. Meanwhile, 500 hPa \nridging is in place across the central CONUS, extending \nnortheastward across the central Great Lakes into southern Quebec,\nsupporting an expansive ~1030 mb surface high over the Ottawa and\nSt. Lawrence valleys. Eastern Kentucky lies along the southern \nperiphery of the high at the start of the period, under a drying \nnortheasterly flow.\n\nThis pattern remains fairly stagnant heading through the weekend--\nthe coastal low becomes vertically stacked off the Mid-Atlantic and\nNew England coast. Meanwhile, the upper-level ridging over Canada\nbreaks away to the northeast while the central CONUS ridge \nremains. As a result, the surface high splits and weakens, with \nthe more southerly portion settling southward to over the Ohio \nValley and central Appalachians by Monday as its associated air \nmass modifies at lower latitudes. As that high scoots toward the \nAtlantic Seaboard on Tuesday, southwesterly return flow will \ndevelop in its wake, bringing warmth and deeper moisture back to \nthe region.\n\nIn sensible weather terms, look for an extended period of mostly\nfair skies, dry northeasterly breezes, cool temperatures, and\nnighttime river valley fog from Thursday night through Sunday. High\ntemperatures will be delightful mild in the upper 60s to mid 70s \neach afternoon. Nighttime lows will turn cool, a bit chilly even, \nsettling deep into the mid 40s to mid 50s Thursday night and deep \ninto the 40s Friday and Saturday nights. A warming trend does \nensue by early next week, with daytime highs recovering into the \nupper 70s to mid 80s by Tuesday and lows moderating back to near \n50F, but it should remain mostly dry.",
